en elementary row operations.\n\n- The first operation is ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second operation is ${R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+8{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+8{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3302cac0-c190-588a-bf1e-1dcf4b11b461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429753} {:answer/id #uuid "36398b63-dad4-5688-8a66-fe0da922b660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429754} {:answer/id #uuid "432a4bca-b02b-564d-8440-b892a8cece98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429755} {:answer/id #uuid "892d14b4-33ce-5d53-88a8-6c11d10a6e6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429759} {:answer/id #uuid "c96b69bc-01bd-5856-808f-355764eb3024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c96b69bc-01bd-5856-808f-355764eb3024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429766} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa023706-5551-57d6-a234-fed6508928d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a861accd-0de4-505d-8847-8b30f8b4e546" :question/math-academy-id "q-110850" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$  by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{3}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{3}=0\\cdot {a}_{1}+(-1)\\cdot {a}_{2}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{3}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "316d5931-7999-53f3-ad90-06c5ea96ca28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488576} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc5baa7-58c6-5fb1-bb5b-bc5d2809c3fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488596} {:answer/id #uuid "81e78e16-327e-5283-9ae6-8297686d3018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488728} {:answer/id #uuid "8961892e-bd58-5202-80e6-b3295e996c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488743} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4f12a7-86f7-5632-aa12-4ebf32e0df03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4f12a7-86f7-5632-aa12-4ebf32e0df03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488756} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af92bdb-0264-5972-9c25-894d9f25aaee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c69f867a-804d-5cf3-bc38-00ede80f0b28" :question/math-academy-id "q-110853" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find a subset of linearly independent vectors. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033e49db-c9d2-5b07-8897-0a4bd99e26ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244337} {:answer/id #uuid "2d130ad5-782d-524d-b31f-3f3c552e7760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244719} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6b5774-4554-5a55-95f0-290cd2754c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245140} {:answer/id #uuid "aafb0663-a194-5ece-b553-6d5258d4281c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245840} {:answer/id #uuid "fbc8a320-650d-598f-9177-752d0f8c20a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fbc8a320-650d-598f-9177-752d0f8c20a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246521} :answer-field/id #uuid "581e042d-203f-58ac-b772-18f562dc056b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "596db895-6072-51ec-b248-ca9f56bec30c" :question/math-academy-id "q-110972" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} &=-9 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3} &=-29 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 6 & -29 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 & 6\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 2 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=-1,$ ${x}_{2}=-2,$ and ${x}_{3}=-3,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+(-2)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-3)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ -29 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=(-1)+(-2)+(-3)=-6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265862 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37062ec7-152b-5164-a3d0-355f27db1080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263493} {:answer/id #uuid "40fdd066-50a9-56e1-9f21-38c1bf8770b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263575} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4047fd-6f9f-5f76-8868-a821b02a6022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263661} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad76de-ec95-57ff-9298-b293f98b1ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264951} {:answer/id #uuid "fc48ca66-d443-578e-9379-fa3ec3e3dd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad76de-ec95-57ff-9298-b293f98b1ef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264951} :answer-field/id #uuid "241201b7-ee2f-5818-8a2b-dbb615d80d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "797519b5-ee4f-5bc0-bbbc-671debb4c9ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-130243" :question/problem "Calculate the product $A{I}_{2}$ given that ${I}_{2}$ is the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix and\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Any matrix, when multiplied by an identity matrix, remains unchanged (assuming that the multiplication is well defined).\n\nThe matrix $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and ${I}_{2}$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix too. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication is well defined and \n\n$$\nA\\cdot {I}_{2}=A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265864 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13fee5aa-433b-519c-a879-d278aa8a48f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263188} {:answer/id #uuid "41aaa268-3659-536e-98cb-3962c98cba4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263581} {:answer/id #uuid "4b7ae9d8-1791-5400-b198-890be82afcf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263650} {:answer/id #uuid "51369c57-0490-5682-9521-7d30dfd3b175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263705} {:answer/id #uuid "b8683bd6-23f6-5798-b795-d12c220d0f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13fee5aa-433b-519c-a879-d278aa8a48f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263188} :answer-field/id #uuid "44b24809-0a0e-5c7b-9ffd-2ce793b20dcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2038a353-9df5-5f59-a4d0-04cb2cfa4cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-130247" :question/problem "Find the product $AB$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $B$ is the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $A$ by the $2$nd *column* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3},$ the result must be the $2$nd *column* of $A.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nAB=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\left(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
